WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:05.463
The pull request that changed the invoice extractor had one line.

00:00:05.763 --> 00:00:11.429
Extract the invoice total became: extract the invoice total, with two decimal

00:00:11.429 --> 00:00:11.944
places.

00:00:12.594 --> 00:00:17.411
The description said: format tweak, tested on three invoices.

00:00:17.711 --> 00:00:19.031
The reviewer approved it.

00:00:19.331 --> 00:00:21.041
The change looked harmless.

00:00:21.691 --> 00:00:27.396
A week later, accuracy on the total field for discounted invoices had dropped.

00:00:27.696 --> 00:00:30.775
And nobody knew since when, or by how much.

00:00:31.075 --> 00:00:34.548
Because there was no number from before to compare against.

00:00:35.198 --> 00:00:36.283
Here's what happened.

00:00:36.583 --> 00:00:40.164
The three test invoices were the ones that motivated the change.

00:00:40.464 --> 00:00:42.438
Round totals, no discount.

00:00:43.088 --> 00:00:48.473
To force two decimal places, the model started combining the goods value and the

00:00:48.473 --> 00:00:49.078
discount.

00:00:49.728 --> 00:00:54.157
And the total came out wrong, on exactly the invoices the team never looked at.

00:00:54.457 --> 00:00:59.093
In this lesson, you'll learn to recognize this smell, and the order of work that

00:00:59.093 --> 00:00:59.731
removes it.

00:01:00.931 --> 00:01:02.735
The cycle looks like this.

00:01:03.035 --> 00:01:06.191
Someone changes the prompt to fix one case.

00:01:06.841 --> 00:01:09.939
They test with the few examples they have on hand.

00:01:10.589 --> 00:01:14.047
The change ships, with no comparison against the previous version.

00:01:14.697 --> 00:01:17.462
The effect shows up on cases nobody tested.

00:01:18.112 --> 00:01:20.146
And users discover the regression.

00:01:20.446 --> 00:01:22.150
Late, and with no data.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:23.711
How do you spot it?

00:01:24.011 --> 00:01:28.451
Prompt changes go through code review as if they were text, with no results

00:01:28.451 --> 00:01:28.984
attached.

00:01:29.634 --> 00:01:34.209
The test for a change is: I ran a few examples, and it looked fine.

00:01:34.859 --> 00:01:39.098
There's no set of cases with expected answers stored in the repository.

00:01:39.748 --> 00:01:42.526
Nobody can state the system's current accuracy.

00:01:42.826 --> 00:01:44.173
Only that it works.

00:01:44.473 --> 00:01:48.909
And switching models keeps getting postponed, because nobody knows what will break.

00:01:49.559 --> 00:01:54.175
And production bugs get fixed in the prompt, and never become test cases.

00:01:55.375 --> 00:01:58.147
A prompt looks like text, not code.

00:01:58.447 --> 00:02:01.256
Changing a sentence doesn't feel like changing logic.

00:02:01.556 --> 00:02:04.213
But with a model, a sentence is logic.

00:02:04.513 --> 00:02:08.202
With two decimal places changed the total field.

00:02:08.852 --> 00:02:11.259
Building a dataset is work.

00:02:11.559 --> 00:02:16.614
Collecting and labeling fifty cases seems expensive next to a one line change.

00:02:16.914 --> 00:02:19.642
The cost shows up later, in the silent regression.

00:02:20.292 --> 00:02:21.687
Output varies.

00:02:21.987 --> 00:02:27.264
Not knowing how to test something that isn't deterministic, the team gives up on

00:02:27.264 --> 00:02:27.792
testing.

00:02:28.092 --> 00:02:32.702
But a grading rule, like comparing fields, covers most of it.

00:02:33.352 --> 00:02:35.708
And the bug's cases become the only test.

00:02:36.008 --> 00:02:38.893
Whoever fixes a bug tests the case that broke.

00:02:39.193 --> 00:02:40.173
And nothing else.

00:02:41.373 --> 00:02:44.054
The first cost is the silent regression.

00:02:44.354 --> 00:02:49.242
Quality drops on part of the cases, and nobody notices until the cost shows up

00:02:49.242 --> 00:02:50.182
somewhere else.

00:02:50.482 --> 00:02:51.872
A wrong payment slip.

00:02:52.172 --> 00:02:54.183
Tax computed on the wrong total.

00:02:54.483 --> 00:02:56.864
A seller with a rejected invoice.

00:02:57.514 --> 00:02:58.715
Then fear.

00:02:59.015 --> 00:03:03.788
With no safety net, the team avoids improving the prompt, or switching models, even

00:03:03.788 --> 00:03:05.226
when it would be cheaper.

00:03:05.876 --> 00:03:07.427
Then endless debates.

00:03:07.727 --> 00:03:11.384
It got better, versus it got worse, with no number to settle it.

00:03:12.034 --> 00:03:13.681
And bugs that come back.

00:03:13.981 --> 00:03:18.235
The error fixed in March returns in June, because nothing stops it.

00:03:18.885 --> 00:03:22.372
And here's how the hole hides, with illustrative numbers.

00:03:22.672 --> 00:03:26.966
Two hundred invoices in the eval, six of them with a discount.

00:03:27.266 --> 00:03:31.327
The overall pass rate drops from ninety-seven to ninety-four percent.

00:03:31.627 --> 00:03:32.878
It looks like noise.

00:03:33.178 --> 00:03:37.143
But in the discount category, six out of six now fail.

00:03:37.443 --> 00:03:41.776
The average hid exactly the hole that finance is going to find.

00:03:42.976 --> 00:03:44.449
Here's one for you.

00:03:44.749 --> 00:03:50.427
You have no eval at all, and you need to fix an extractor that gets totals wrong, by

00:03:50.427 --> 00:03:51.035
tomorrow.

00:03:51.335 --> 00:03:54.349
What's the first step you still have time to do?

00:03:57.399 --> 00:04:00.575
Label the fifteen invoices you already know break.

00:04:00.875 --> 00:04:03.593
Measure the current prompt on them.

00:04:03.893 --> 00:04:04.860
Then change it.

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:09.095
Fifteen cases with expected answers already are an eval.

00:04:09.395 --> 00:04:11.719
Measuring first gives you the floor.

00:04:12.019 --> 00:04:17.070
And those fifteen go into the repository, and run on the pull request.

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:22.579
Shipping now and filing a card to add an eval next sprint doesn't work.

00:04:22.879 --> 00:04:25.388
The card sits behind the next fix.

00:04:25.688 --> 00:04:31.237
And asking a model to generate two hundred synthetic invoices as ground truth misses

00:04:31.237 --> 00:04:33.550
the crooked discount of a real one.

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:36.349
Start with what already failed in production.

00:04:37.549 --> 00:04:42.073
The treatment is a refactoring called add eval before changing the prompt.

00:04:42.373 --> 00:04:43.827
And the order matters.

00:04:44.127 --> 00:04:46.461
It's the part teams invert most often.

00:04:47.111 --> 00:04:50.185
First, store cases with expected answers.

00:04:50.485 --> 00:04:54.657
Start with production bugs, and a couple of dozen happy examples.

00:04:54.957 --> 00:04:56.474
Split them by category.

00:04:56.774 --> 00:05:00.534
With discount, missing line item, a rare tax code.

00:05:01.184 --> 00:05:03.332
Measure the current version.

00:05:03.632 --> 00:05:05.315
That number is the floor.

00:05:05.615 --> 00:05:08.360
Without it, every it got better is just talk.

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:11.904
Change the prompt, or the model, on a branch.

00:05:12.554 --> 00:05:13.528
Measure again.

00:05:13.828 --> 00:05:16.650
Look at the overall rate and the rate per category.

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:21.974
A small category at zero is a regression, even if the average held still.

00:05:22.624 --> 00:05:24.774
And the case that broke goes into the set.

00:05:25.074 --> 00:05:26.454
Otherwise it comes back.

00:05:27.104 --> 00:05:32.787
And in your pipeline, the eval runs on every pull request that touches a prompt, a

00:05:32.787 --> 00:05:34.243
template, or a model.

00:05:34.543 --> 00:05:36.071
That last part matters.

00:05:36.371 --> 00:05:41.413
A team with a forty case eval that hasn't run in four months, with half the cases in

00:05:41.413 --> 00:05:43.574
an old format, still has this smell.

00:05:43.874 --> 00:05:46.432
An eval that doesn't run protects nothing.

00:05:47.632 --> 00:05:49.143
Some close relatives.

00:05:49.443 --> 00:05:54.849
Before each deploy, someone asks the bot the same eight questions and eyeballs the

00:05:54.849 --> 00:05:55.376
answers.

00:05:55.676 --> 00:05:57.730
That's a ritual, not a measurement.

00:05:58.030 --> 00:06:00.685
It has its own name: vibe check eval.

00:06:01.335 --> 00:06:06.591
A team runs two hundred cases on every change, but only looks at the overall rate.

00:06:06.891 --> 00:06:08.179
That's not this smell.

00:06:08.479 --> 00:06:09.931
The eval exists.

00:06:10.231 --> 00:06:14.904
It just needs results per category, and an alert when any of them drops.

00:06:15.554 --> 00:06:20.655
And a production bug, total ignores discount, fixed in the prompt.

00:06:20.955 --> 00:06:25.788
The pull request passed a two hundred case eval that had no discounted invoice at

00:06:25.788 --> 00:06:26.027
all.

00:06:26.327 --> 00:06:28.476
It passed because it couldn't see the bug.

00:06:28.776 --> 00:06:31.641
Add the invoice that broke, and others like it.

00:06:32.841 --> 00:06:34.443
When can you live without it?

00:06:34.743 --> 00:06:36.999
A prototype, in its first week.

00:06:37.299 --> 00:06:41.562
Before you know what the system should do, testing by hand is reasonable.

00:06:41.862 --> 00:06:45.189
Start the dataset when the first real user shows up.

00:06:45.839 --> 00:06:50.839
And a personal script that summarizes your meetings can live without an eval.

00:06:51.139 --> 00:06:53.732
Once someone else depends on it, it can't.

00:06:54.932 --> 00:06:56.730
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:06:57.030 --> 00:06:59.811
With a model, a sentence is logic.

00:07:00.111 --> 00:07:02.915
Treat every prompt change like a code change.

00:07:03.215 --> 00:07:09.335
With stored cases, a number from before, a number from after, and the broken case

00:07:09.335 --> 00:07:10.619
added to the set.

00:07:10.919 --> 00:07:15.796
Then switching models becomes a measured decision, made in an afternoon.

00:07:16.446 --> 00:07:22.459
In the next lesson, we'll do that refactoring step by step: add eval before changing

00:07:22.459 --> 00:07:23.246
the prompt.

00:07:23.546 --> 00:07:24.376
See you there.
